.SH "BUGS"
The \fBparse_before_double_dash\fR function calls \fBsplit(a, "=", limit=2)\fR unconditionally when \fB\-\-assignment\-operator\fR or \fB\-\-argument\-types\-library\fR is detected, but then tests the variable \fBval\fR (undefined) rather than \fBvalue\fR, meaning the \fB=\fR\-form of these options will always raise an \fBUndefVarError\fR at runtime\.
.P
Similarly, in the \fB\-a=\fR and \fB\-l=\fR short\-option handlers, the parsed value is assigned to \fBval\fR (undefined) rather than \fBassignment_operator\fR or \fBarguments_path\fR\.
.P
The \fB\-\-unix\-flags\fR handler sets \fBassignment_operator = "="\fR at module level but \fBparse_before_double_dash\fR also returns a local \fBunix_flags\fR/\fBassignment_operator\fR; the interaction between the global and local variables may produce unexpected behaviour\.
.P
In the main passthrough loop, the variable \fBassingment\fR (a typo for \fBassignment\fR) is written in several branches but the correctly\-spelled \fBassignment\fR is what is passed to \fBpreprocessor\fR, so the space\-separated assignment operator is silently discarded\.
.P
The \fB>\fR redirection handler contains a bare \fBname =\fR with no right\-hand side (line 800), which is a syntax error that will prevent the script from loading\.
.P
The \fBtrace_file_io\fR function contains two debugging \fBprintln\fR calls (\fB"pickle"\fR and \fB"pockle"\fR) that are unconditionally written to standard output and will corrupt the \fB$ARGS\fR fragment when \fB\-\-run\fR is used\.
.P
The output\-types library file is opened in append mode; re\-running the script for the same \fB$PROG\fR will duplicate entries rather than replacing them\.
.P
Multi\-arity argument handling is not yet implemented; the relevant \fB\-\-arity\fR and \fB\-\-separator\fR lines in \fBpreprocessor\fR are commented out with a \fBTODO\fR note\.
